welcome back to cbs this morning  hurricane sally is expected to make  landfall as a category one tomorrow  producing heavy rain and a potentially  life-threatening storm surge states of  emergency have already been declared in  mississippi  alabama and louisiana and manuel barcas  is in biloxi mississippi for us manny  good morning to you  what's it feel like there well it  definitely  is starting to get worse the weather  here in biloxi and i can tell you that  the window to evacuate some of these  low-lying  areas will begin to close later today  and that includes here where the storm  surge  could be up to nine feet the city of  biloxi declared a state  of emergency and opened up sandbag  locations for residents who would like  to shore up their properties some told  us that they saw the forecast track  and they realize this is not the kind of  storm that they want to take  any chances with what is perhaps most  telling  is the fact that the fire department  here is preparing  for water rescues and that's because yes  the winds will be something to contend  with  as the storm comes ashore but they're  really concerned about the combination  of the storm surge the heavy rainfall  and the fact that the storm could just  hover  over this area for quite some time that  could likely  lead to life-threatening flooding not  just here  in biloxi but across a wide swath of the  gulf coast  gail thank you manuel
 

